00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:48.135
The first step in this case is
going to be silver in this case.

00:15:49.485 --> 00:15:54.705
So if we put a plus one charge, zinc will
be the second one, which is plus two.

00:15:55.705 --> 00:15:57.695
Aluminum will be the third step.

00:15:58.215 --> 00:16:03.140
So usually what I, where I always start
the steps is aluminum being that It's

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:05.520
kind of on that third column there.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.530
So it will help you kind of remember
where the plus three generally starts.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:13.750
And then you're just walking
down the steps as you go.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.940
So it goes three, two, one.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:21.300
And now these are all the predictable
charges that are always the same for ions.

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.679
They always have this tendency
to lose or gain electrons.

00:16:25.319 --> 00:16:27.819
So the right hand side is your anions.

00:16:28.079 --> 00:16:30.700
The left hand side is your cations.

00:16:31.194 --> 00:16:35.995
So you can kind of see metals
and then your non metals have

00:16:36.064 --> 00:16:38.785
these general abilities here.

